{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794825424,"nonce":"VCKR8u67ghZM4x+mQKcxwSW40I8=","message":"Seventh nAIR slice, e6ab184d: string literals. genhl does not allocate a string where one is written: make_string reads a global that holds a shared String object from the module's constants table. nAIR now does the same: TConst String is GetGlobal of a global typed String, one global per distinct text, and Module.constants lists them (global and string-pool index) for the runtime to build at start. ModuleInfo gained string_value, string_pool_len and string_constant (the text a constant global holds); AshModule answers them from the bytecode's constants, NairModule from its own, and the agreement test compares every literal's text per function with genhl's, which is what showed my first version (a New and two FieldSets per evaluation) was wrong. Cranelift reads its string constants through the module now. The reference interpreter has globals and builds the constants, and a test reads the literals back, with UTF-16 lengths for a non-ASCII text, as lowered and after O2 and O3. A real defect found on the way: a function's int pool base was the pool length when that function finished lowering, so a function lowered before its callees (size, before greeting and pick) had a base below the callees' constants and inlining read them as ones a pass had minted; the base is now the final pool length, set in lower_all and again in NairModule. Next: static variables and the init function that builds the statics objects and runs the static initializers in program order, then exceptions, abstracts and the hl.Bytes natives.","files":null}]}